course: dictionary
lesson: 1298
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The clues in the sentence show which meaning the writer means. Bypass is a good example: it can be a road, a heart operation, or a verb meaning to go around something.
explain: «A motorway has no crossings and no traffic lights, only gentle bends, several lanes and slip roads to join and leave; the first UK motorway, the Preston Bypass, opened in 1958.» The clues are motorway, lanes and slip roads, so a bypass here is a road that goes around a town instead of through it.
explain: Used as a verb, bypass means to go around something to avoid it, or to avoid a rule or system. Try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: A bypass is a road that goes around a town instead of through it.
fact: In medicine, a heart bypass is an operation that makes a new path for blood to flow around a blocked part of the heart's blood vessels.
fact: As a verb, bypass means to go around something or someone to avoid it.
fact: Bypass can also mean to ignore a rule, or to avoid using a system.
fact: Useful phrases: "heart bypass", "take the bypass", "bypass the rules".
exercise: In «the first UK motorway, the Preston Bypass, opened in 1958», what is the Preston Bypass? | A river crossing for boats | *A road that goes around a town | A hospital for people with heart problems | A school for drivers
exercise: A nurse says, «The heart bypass gave his blood a new path.» What is a heart bypass? | A test to see how fast you run | A bandage for a sore arm | A type of heart-shaped sweet | *An operation that makes a new path for blood
exercise: «Ravi decided to bypass the busy town centre.» What does bypass mean here? | To paint it bright red | *To go around it to avoid it | To stop and shop there | To ask the way through it
test: «Mia tried to bypass the rules and get in early.» What does bypass suggest here? | To write new rules for the school | To read the rules out loud | To follow every rule very carefully | *To ignore a rule
test: Which meaning of bypass fits «The lorries take the bypass to avoid the village»? | A rule that lorries must obey | A loud horn for warning people | *A road that goes around the village | An operation on the lorries
exam: Which sentence uses bypass as a verb? | The surgeon explained the heart bypass in simple words. | The town put up a sign for the new bypass. | *They chose to bypass the traffic jam by taking the back roads. | The bypass opened last year and cut the journey in half.
